---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "Best Smart Treadmills (2026): Screens, Apps and What You Keep Paying";
const description =
  "Six smart treadmills across iFIT, Peloton, JRNY, Echelon and open apps, chosen from published specs and warranty terms. What each screen needs a membership for, and what it costs to own.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "nordictrack-commercial-1750",
    name: "NordicTrack Commercial 1750",
    category: "Best overall smart treadmill",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0D5YKF4SK?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: "/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill/",
    priceRange: "Around $1,900–$2,300",
    specs: "16 in. pivoting touchscreen · 0-12 mph · -3% to 12% incline · folding · lifetime motor warranty",
    pros: [
      "The largest iFIT content library, with incline and decline that the platform can drive automatically",
      "A 16-inch screen that pivots, so floor classes off the belt are usable",
      "Decline to -3%, which few folding machines at this price offer",
      "NordicTrack covers the motor for life, the clearest durability signal in its range",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Most of what makes it smart — classes, automatic control, workout history — sits behind a paid iFIT membership",
      "Motor rating, deck width and maximum user weight have changed between model years and are quoted differently across sources",
      "Heavily and constantly discounted, which makes list price a poor guide to value",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The default smart treadmill for someone who wants iFIT and will keep paying for it — buy it on discount, and confirm the current-year specification before ordering.",
    description: "The Commercial 1750 is the middle of NordicTrack's Commercial series and the machine most iFIT buyers end up on. NordicTrack confirms a 16-inch HD touchscreen that pivots, a 0 to 12 mph speed range, incline from -3% to 12%, a folding frame and a lifetime motor warranty. Those are the figures that agree across NordicTrack's page, a major retailer listing and independent coverage. The motor rating, the deck width and the maximum user weight are a different story: they have changed between model years and different sources quote different numbers, so we have left them out — our 1250 vs 1750 comparison sets out the conflict. What makes the 1750 the best overall pick is the platform rather than the hardware alone. iFIT can change speed and incline for you during a class, including on outdoor-route workouts that use the decline, and the pivoting screen makes the non-running content usable. Without a membership it drops back to a manual treadmill with a large, mostly idle screen.",
  },
  {
    id: "nordictrack-t-series-10",
    name: "NordicTrack T Series 10",
    category: "Best budget treadmill with a built-in screen",
    badge: "Best Budget",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0D7XGWMRD?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: "/nordictrack-t-series-treadmill/",
    priceRange: "Around $1,200–$1,600",
    specs: "20 x 60 in. deck · 10 in. tilting touchscreen · 0-12% incline · up to 12 mph · 325 lb capacity",
    pros: [
      "A full 60-inch running length and a built-in touchscreen at the lowest price in the NordicTrack range",
      "Same iFIT platform and automatic incline and speed control as the more expensive machines",
      "Adjustable SelectFlex cushioning and a fold-up frame",
      "A 325 lb maximum user weight that NordicTrack, a retailer and independent reviewers all agree on",
    ],
    cons: [
      "No decline, and a narrower 20-inch belt than the Commercial series",
      "Independent reviewers describe the free tier as limited to manual mode and a handful of workouts",
      "Motor cover is two years, against lifetime on the Commercial 1750",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The cheapest way into a built-in iFIT screen without giving up a 60-inch deck — a good fit for walkers and moderate runners who want the classes.",
    description: "The T Series 10 is NordicTrack's entry-level folding treadmill and the cheapest way into its range. NordicTrack, a major retailer and two independent reviewers agree on a 20 by 60-inch belt, a 10-inch tilting touchscreen, 0 to 12% incline, a top speed of 12 mph and a 325 lb maximum user weight. The motor rating is where the sources part: NordicTrack lists 3.0 CHP, while a retailer listing describes a 3.6 CHP drive system rated down to 3.0, so we have not put a figure in the spec line. NordicTrack's own listing puts the motor and parts on two-year cover with ten years on the frame and one year of labour. As a smart treadmill it gets the same iFIT platform as the Commercial machines, including automatic control of speed and incline during classes. Without a paid membership, independent reviews describe a manual mode, a free account with a few template workouts and a featured class, and no entertainment apps — which is a fair summary of what the free tier of any iFIT machine offers.",
  },
  {
    id: "peloton-tread-vision",
    name: "Peloton Tread Vision",
    category: "Best for Peloton classes and form feedback",
    badge: "Best for Peloton",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0HDRFM27D?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "Around $3,500",
    specs: "23.8 in. swivel touchscreen · 0-12.5 mph · 0-12.5% incline · non-folding",
    pros: [
      "Peloton's instructor-led running, walking and strength classes, which are the platform's main draw",
      "A 23.8-inch screen that swivels for off-belt strength and yoga classes",
      "Movement-tracking camera for running-form and rep feedback, new on this generation",
      "Speed and incline knobs with jump buttons for instant interval changes",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Launched on 1 October 2026, so there is no long-term independent coverage of reliability yet",
      "The form-tracking, personalised plans and classes are membership features, and the membership is ongoing",
      "Does not fold, and its 59-inch belt is a little shorter than the 60 inches on most picks here",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For someone who already knows they want Peloton classes and will keep the All-Access Membership; nobody else should pay this much for the screen.",
    description: "Peloton replaced its treadmill line in autumn 2026 with three machines: the folding Tread Flex, the Tread Vision and the slat-belt Tread+ Vision. The Tread Vision is the one most buyers will consider. Peloton's specification page and its launch material agree on a 23.8-inch swivel touchscreen, 0 to 12.5 mph and 0 to 12.5% incline, and the machine does not fold; Peloton lists a 59 by 20-inch cushioned belt and a 300 lb maximum user weight. Its new feature is a movement-tracking camera that scores running efficiency from cadence and vertical oscillation and counts reps in strength classes. Peloton prices the hardware with the All-Access Membership sold separately and says the membership is required for its content and features; its shop page also describes a Just Run mode for running without a class. Because the machine has only been on sale since 1 October, we cannot point to independent reliability coverage, and the warranty — 12 months limited, with longer belt, motor and frame guarantees — is shorter in parts than the lifetime motor cover several rivals here offer.",
  },
  {
    id: "echelon-stride-6s",
    name: "Echelon Stride-6s",
    category: "Best smart treadmill for small spaces",
    badge: "Best Auto-Fold",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Echelon+Stride-6s+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "Around $1,700–$2,300",
    specs: "20.5 x 60 in. deck · 10 in. touchscreen · auto-fold · 300 lb capacity",
    pros: [
      "Folds itself at the press of a button and stores in a small footprint, which no other pick here does",
      "A full 60-inch running length despite the compact design",
      "Built-in touchscreen runs Echelon classes without a separate tablet",
      "Usually cheaper than the other built-in-screen picks outside NordicTrack",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Independent reviews report that Echelon has required a membership with the purchase, and without one only a manual mode remains",
      "Published top speed and warranty terms differ between Echelon and independent reviewers, so confirm both",
      "A 10-inch screen is small for a machine sold on its classes",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The pick for an apartment or shared room where the treadmill has to disappear after every session — and where Echelon classes are the plan.",
    description: "The Stride-6s is Echelon's compact smart treadmill, and its defining feature is the auto-fold: press a button and the deck folds flat against the frame, leaving a footprint small enough to stand against a wall. Echelon's listing and independent coverage agree on a 20.5 by 60-inch deck, a 10-inch touchscreen and a 300 lb maximum user weight, and Echelon lists powered incline across twelve levels. We left the top speed out of the spec line because Echelon quotes 12.5 mph while two independent reviews list 12.4, and we left the warranty out because Echelon's current page advertises a four-year guarantee while an earlier independent review gives different frame and motor terms. The subscription point matters more here than on most machines: an independent review reports that Echelon has required at least a month of its Premier membership with the purchase, and that after cancelling only a manual FreeStyle mode remains. Ask what the current terms are before you buy.",
  },
  {
    id: "bowflex-t6",
    name: "Bowflex T6",
    category: "Best for JRNY or your own tablet",
    badge: "Best Value",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Bowflex+T6+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: "/bowflex-t6-treadmill/",
    priceRange: "$899–$1,299",
    specs: "20 x 60 in. deck · 3.0 HP (test rating) · 0-15% incline · 0.5-12 mph · 325 lb capacity",
    pros: [
      "No subscription needed to use the treadmill; JRNY is optional rather than built into the console",
      "Bluetooth connection to JRNY, Peloton or Zwift on your own tablet",
      "Lifetime frame and motor warranty, plus three years on mechanical parts",
      "Incline to 15% and a full 60-inch deck at the lowest price here",
    ],
    cons: [
      "No built-in touchscreen — the screen is whichever tablet you bring",
      "The 3.0 HP figure is a test rating, not a continuous-duty figure, so it does not compare directly with CHP",
      "One year of in-home labour is short next to the lifetime frame and motor cover",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The best-value way to get a smart treadmill experience: buy a capable machine and use the tablet and app you already have.",
    description: "The T6 is the cheapest treadmill on this list and arguably the most flexible, because its intelligence lives on your tablet rather than in a built-in screen. The figures are Bowflex's own for the T6, cross-checked in our review: a 20 by 60-inch deck, 0 to 15% incline, 0.5 to 12 mph, a 325 lb maximum user weight and a drive motor Bowflex rates at 3.0 HP under test settings — which, as Bowflex itself notes, is not the same thing as a continuous-duty rating. The console is a dual LED and LCD layout with Bluetooth for JRNY, Peloton and Zwift. JRNY is Bowflex's own adaptive-workout platform and is a paid membership, but it is optional, and nothing on the console needs it to work. The warranty is lifetime on the frame and motor, three years on mechanical parts and one year of labour. On 13 October 2026 bowflex.com was selling it below its regular price, which is the price to wait for.",
  },
  {
    id: "horizon-7-8-at",
    name: "Horizon 7.8 AT",
    category: "Best smart treadmill with no subscription",
    badge: "No Subscription",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Horizon+7.8+AT+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "Around $2,000–$2,700",
    specs: "22 x 60 in. deck · 0.5-12 mph · 0-15% incline · 375 lb capacity · lifetime frame and motor",
    pros: [
      "Built around open apps — Bluetooth to Zwift, Peloton and Kinomap — with no platform of its own to pay for",
      "A 22 by 60-inch deck, the widest here alongside the NordicTrack Commercial series",
      "Lifetime frame and motor, five years on parts and two years of labour",
      "A 375 lb maximum user weight, the highest on this list",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The 9.3-inch colour display is a data screen, not a touchscreen, so classes run on your own tablet",
      "Horizon does not publish a drive-motor rating; the figures quoted elsewhere are not confirmed by the manufacturer",
      "No automatic incline or speed control from the built-in console in the way iFIT provides",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The right machine for someone who wants app connectivity without being tied to one platform — and who would rather spend the subscription money on a better treadmill.",
    description: "The 7.8 AT is the top of Horizon's Studio Series, and Horizon's pitch for it is explicitly anti-lock-in: no built-in content platform, and Bluetooth that connects to whichever app you prefer. Horizon's specification page and independent reviews agree on a 22 by 60-inch cushioned deck, 0.5 to 12 mph, 0 to 15% incline and a 375 lb maximum user weight, with a lifetime frame and motor warranty, five years on parts and two years of labour. Horizon does not state a drive-motor rating, and the 4.0 figure quoted by some reviewers is the reviewers' own research rather than a manufacturer specification, so we have left it out. The console is a 9.3-inch colour display with Bluetooth speakers and tablet holders, and Horizon includes a Bluetooth chest strap. On 13 October 2026 Horizon's own store showed it below its regular price but shipping later in the month, so check stock. If you want guided classes, you bring the app and pay for it — but you can change apps without changing treadmills.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Best Treadmills", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Best Smart Treadmills", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-smart-treadmills/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is a smart treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A smart treadmill connects to software that does more than show speed and distance. Usually that means a built-in touchscreen running a content platform such as iFIT or Peloton, which can stream classes and change speed and incline for you. More loosely, it also covers treadmills that connect over Bluetooth to apps such as Zwift on your own tablet.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do smart treadmills require a subscription?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The classes and automatic control on built-in platforms do, and that membership is an ongoing cost for as long as you use them. The treadmill itself usually still runs in a manual mode without it. Bowflex and Horizon machines need no subscription to work at all; you pay for an app only if you want one. Always check the current terms for the exact model.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a built-in screen better than using a tablet?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It is more integrated, not necessarily better. A built-in screen makes automatic speed and incline changes seamless and suits classes that move off the belt. A tablet is cheaper, gets replaced as technology moves on, and lets you change apps without changing treadmills. If automatic control matters most, choose a built-in platform; if flexibility matters most, choose a tablet setup.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I use Zwift on a NordicTrack or Peloton?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Those machines are designed to run their own platforms, and connecting a third-party app ranges from awkward to unsupported depending on the model and software version. If Zwift matters to you, choose a treadmill with a standard Bluetooth fitness connection, such as the Bowflex T6 or Horizon 7.8 AT, or confirm compatibility for the exact model before buying.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What happens to a smart treadmill if the company stops supporting it?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The motor, deck and frame keep working, and most machines retain a manual mode. What you lose is the screen-based content and, eventually, software updates. That is why it is worth weighting warranty terms and the underlying hardware heavily, and why tablet-based machines age more gracefully: you simply replace the tablet.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best smart treadmill for a small apartment?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Of these six, the Echelon Stride-6s is the one built for it: it folds itself flat at the press of a button and stores in a small footprint, with a 10-inch touchscreen. The NordicTrack T Series 10 and Bowflex T6 also fold. Check the folded dimensions against your space and confirm the delivery route before ordering.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-10-13",
      dateModified: "2026-10-13",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.name.split(" ")[0] },
      offers: {
        "@type": "AggregateOffer",
        priceCurrency: "USD",
        availability: "https://schema.org/InStock",
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-10-13",
    modifiedTime: "2026-10-13",
    author: "Morgan Reyes",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best smart treadmill",
      "treadmill with screen",
      "ifit treadmill",
      "peloton tread",
      "treadmill with zwift",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See Our Top Picks", link: "#picks" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-bestsmarttreadmills" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-bestsmarttreadmills)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Buying Guide
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated October 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best Smart Treadmills <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Screens, Apps and the Ongoing Cost</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          A smart treadmill is two purchases: a machine you pay for once and a platform you pay for every month you use it. These six cover every major ecosystem, and for each one we say what the screen does when the membership stops.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#picks" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            See the Picks
          </a>
          <a href="#subscription-lock-in" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            What Works Without a Subscription
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">October 13, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="2.7k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">How we built this list.</strong> On 13 October 2026 we
          checked each machine against its manufacturer's current product or specification page,
          at least one major retailer listing, and independent review coverage. Only figures that
          agreed across those sources appear in the spec lines; where they conflicted — motor
          ratings on the NordicTrack machines, top speed and warranty on the Echelon, motor rating
          on the Horizon — we left the figure out. The Bowflex T6 figures are carried over from
          <a href="/bowflex-t6-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">our T6 review</a>,
          which cross-checked them on 8 October 2026. The Peloton Tread Vision went on sale on
          1 October 2026, so its figures come from Peloton's specification page and launch
          coverage only; there is no long-term independent coverage of it yet. None of this is our
          own measurement — we have not tested these machines. Price bands reflect prices seen on
          13 October 2026 and change often. Subscription prices are deliberately not quoted
          because they change. Confirm the specification, the warranty and what works without a
          membership for the exact unit before you order. We earn a commission if you buy through
          the Amazon links on this page; it does not change which products we list or what we say
          about them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#quick-picks" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Our Picks at a Glance</a>
          <a href="#picks" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. The 6 Best Smart Treadmills</a>
          <a href="#how-we-chose" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. How We Chose These Treadmills</a>
          <a href="#screen-vs-tablet" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Built-In Screen or Your Own Tablet</a>
          <a href="#subscription-lock-in" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Subscription Lock-In: What Works Without It</a>
          <a href="#auto-adjust" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Auto-Adjusting Incline and Speed: What It Is Worth</a>
          <a href="#open-apps" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Open-App Support: Bluetooth, Zwift and the Rest</a>
          <a href="#total-cost" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Total Cost of Ownership</a>
          <a href="#who-should-skip" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Who Should Buy Something Else</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Every smart treadmill is sold on its screen, and every screen is a doorway to a
          platform that charges a recurring fee. That is not a reason to avoid one — guided
          classes and auto-adjusting incline are genuinely why some people keep training — but it
          does mean the right question is not which screen is biggest. It is which ecosystem you
          want to live in for the next several years, and what the hardware is worth on the day
          you stop paying for it.
        </p>

        <div id="quick-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Our Picks at a Glance</h2>
<p>
            Six machines, one for each of the main ways to get a screen and guided training onto a
            treadmill. Four have a built-in touchscreen tied to a content platform; two use your
            own tablet and an app of your choosing. The table is the short version; the cards below
            have the specifications and the caveats. Worried about the subscription? We cover
            <a href="/can-you-use-nordictrack-without-ifit/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether a NordicTrack works without iFIT</a>.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Pick</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Best for</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Why</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
