      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Treadmills</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these machines. The picks were made from published specifications,
            manufacturer listings, warranty terms and independent coverage, checked on 13 October
            2026. The Bowflex T6 figures come from our own review of it, which cross-checked
            Bowflex's specification against independent sources five days earlier.
          </p>
          <p>
            We started from the ecosystems rather than the machines, because on a smart treadmill
            the platform shapes the ownership experience more than any single specification. That
            gave five routes to cover: iFIT on NordicTrack and ProForm, Peloton, Bowflex's JRNY,
            Echelon's own platform, and open apps over Bluetooth. Within each we picked the
            machine that is currently sold, sensibly specified for the money and backed by
            warranty terms we could find in writing.
          </p>
          <p>
            Then we applied the same rule our model reviews use: a figure only appears in a spec
            line if the manufacturer and at least one other source agree on it. That rule removed
            more than you might expect. The motor ratings on both NordicTrack machines are quoted
            differently by different sources and model years. Echelon and independent reviewers
            disagree on the Stride-6s's top speed and warranty. Horizon does not state a motor
            rating at all. Those figures are absent rather than guessed, and each card says why.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two machines we considered did not make the list. The ProForm Pro 5000 has a strong
            deck for its price, but it is no longer clearly listed on ProForm's own site and retail
            listings describe versions with different screens and specifications; our
            <a href="/proform-pro-5000-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Pro 5000 review</a>
            still covers it if you find one. The NordicTrack T 6.5 S has also dropped off
            NordicTrack's site, and its five-inch display was never much of a smart screen.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="screen-vs-tablet" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Built-In Screen or Your Own Tablet</h2>
<p>
            This is the first fork in the decision, and it is worth settling before you compare
            individual machines. A built-in touchscreen is the polished experience: the classes
            are on the console, the machine knows what the class wants, and nothing needs charging
            or propping up. A tablet holder is the flexible one: the screen is whatever device you
            already own, and the app is whichever you choose.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What a built-in screen does well.</strong> It integrates. On iFIT, Peloton and
            Echelon machines the screen and the motor controller talk directly, which is what makes
            automatic speed and incline changes possible without any pairing. Larger screens that
            swivel or pivot — the Commercial 1750's 16 inches, the Tread Vision's 23.8 — also make
            off-belt strength and mobility classes usable, which is a real part of what those
            memberships offer.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What it costs you.</strong> Three things. The screen is part of the purchase
            price, and the most expensive part of many consoles. It is a computer bolted to a
            frame that should last far longer, and it will age faster than the motor beneath it —
            our
            <a href="/treadmill-console-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">console fault guide</a>
            covers what happens when one fails. And it is usually locked to one platform, so the
            screen is worth most while you subscribe to that platform and least when you stop.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What a tablet setup does well.</strong> It separates the parts that age at
            different speeds. A treadmill frame and motor can run for a decade; a tablet is
            replaced every few years anyway, and the new one runs the current version of the app.
            You can move from one platform to another — Zwift this winter, Peloton's app next year
            — without changing treadmills. The Bowflex T6 and Horizon 7.8 AT are built around this
            approach, and it is why both cost less than a built-in screen of similar hardware.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What it costs you.</strong> Integration. A tablet app can only change the
            treadmill's speed and incline if the treadmill accepts control over Bluetooth and the
            app supports it, and that combination is far from universal. On many machines a
            tablet app reads your speed but leaves you to press the buttons. If automatic control
            is the main reason you want a smart treadmill, a built-in platform is still the more
            reliable way to get it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="subscription-lock-in" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Subscription Lock-In: What Works Without It</h2>
<p>
            The membership on a smart treadmill is not a one-off add-on. It is an ongoing charge for
            as long as you want the classes, and over the life of the machine it can add up to more
            than the hardware did. We do not quote current prices because all of these platforms
            change them, but the shape of the cost is stable: a monthly or annual fee, sometimes
            with a first year bundled in at purchase, renewing automatically at full price after
            that.
          </p>
          <p>
            The useful question is what each machine becomes when you stop paying, and the answers
            differ more than the marketing suggests.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>iFIT machines (NordicTrack, ProForm).</strong> Independent reviews of the T
            Series and the Commercial machines describe the same pattern: without a paid
            membership you get a manual mode where you set speed and incline yourself, and with a
            free iFIT account a handful of template workouts and a featured class. The library,
            automatic control and entertainment apps go. Our
            <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">iFIT guide</a> works
            through what the membership is and is not worth.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Peloton.</strong> Peloton states that the All-Access Membership is required to
            access its content and features, and its shop page describes a Just Run mode for
            running without a class. Peloton's no-membership behaviour has changed before, so
            confirm the current position for the Tread Vision specifically. Our
            <a href="/ifit-vs-peloton/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">iFIT vs Peloton comparison</a>
            sets the two platforms side by side.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Echelon.</strong> This is the one to check most carefully. An independent
            review reports that Echelon has required at least one month of membership with the
            purchase, and that afterwards only a manual FreeStyle mode remains.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Bowflex and Horizon.</strong> Neither machine needs any subscription to work as
            a treadmill. Bowflex's JRNY is an optional paid platform; Horizon has no platform of its
            own. You pay for an app only if you want one, and you choose which.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the honest answer is that you would rather not pay a monthly fee at all, a smart
            treadmill is probably the wrong category, and our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a>
            roundup is the better starting point.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="auto-adjust" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Auto-Adjusting Incline and Speed: What It Is Worth</h2>
<p>
            Automatic control is the feature that most separates a smart treadmill from a regular
            one with a tablet shelf. During a class or a route workout, the platform changes the
            belt speed and the deck angle for you, so a hill arrives when the trainer says it does
            and an interval starts without you reaching for a button.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a lot of people that is the whole point. It removes the decisions that make
            treadmill sessions easy to cut short, and it makes structured sessions — hill repeats,
            progressive runs, the incline walks that are the most common use of these machines —
            far easier to follow. iFIT's version extends to outdoor-route workouts where the deck
            follows the elevation of a real course, which is where the Commercial 1750's decline
            earns its place.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three caveats are worth knowing. First, on the built-in platforms automatic control is
            a membership feature; when the membership ends, so does the automatic control.
            Second, you can always override it, and you should — a platform does not know that
            your knee is grumbling today. Third, a machine's incline range limits what the
            platform can do: a 12% ceiling flattens any route steeper than that, and a machine with
            no decline cannot reproduce a descent. If incline is central to how you train, our
            <a href="/best-incline-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">incline treadmill guide</a>
            is worth reading alongside this one.
          </p>
          <p>
            On tablet-based machines, automatic control depends on the app and on whether the
            treadmill accepts commands over Bluetooth. Some app and treadmill combinations change
            speed for you; many only read your speed and leave the controls to you. Check the
            specific pairing before assuming it works.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="open-apps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Open-App Support: Bluetooth, Zwift and the Rest</h2>
<p>
            A treadmill that speaks a standard Bluetooth fitness protocol can send its speed,
            incline and distance to third-party apps on a phone, tablet or laptop. That is what
            lets a Bowflex T6 or a Horizon 7.8 AT run Zwift, the Peloton app or Kinomap without
            either manufacturer's involvement, and it is the single most useful feature to look
            for if you do not want to be tied to one platform.
          </p>
          <p>
            The built-in platforms are much less open. iFIT, Peloton and Echelon machines are
            designed to run their own content on their own screens, and connecting a third-party
            app to them ranges from awkward to unsupported depending on the model and software
            version. If you want the option of Zwift as well as the manufacturer's classes, confirm
            it for the exact machine — our
            <a href="/zwift-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Zwift treadmill guide</a>
            explains how the connection works and what to check.
          </p>
          <p>
            Watch and heart-rate connections are a separate question. Most current smart
            treadmills accept a Bluetooth heart-rate strap, and several connect to watches; the
            Bowflex T6 supports tap-to-connect for Apple Watch and Samsung watches, and Peloton
            lists Apple Watch, Wear OS, Fitbit, Garmin and WHOOP. Whether your watch records the
            session accurately is covered in our
            <a href="/how-to-connect-apple-watch-to-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Apple Watch and treadmill guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="total-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Total Cost of Ownership</h2>
<p>
            Comparing smart treadmills on sticker price is the single most common mistake in this
            category, because the sticker is only the first payment. A fair comparison adds up four
            things over the years you expect to own the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The hardware, at the price you will actually pay.</strong> Most machines on
            this list were selling below their regular price on 13 October 2026, and NordicTrack in
            particular discounts constantly. Our
            <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to when to buy</a>
            covers the sale calendar.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The membership, for every year you use it.</strong> Multiply the current
            annual price by the number of years you expect to train with classes, and assume it
            will rise rather than fall. On a built-in-screen machine this line is often larger
            than the hardware over five years. On a tablet machine it is whatever app you choose,
            including nothing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Delivery, assembly and the warranty you can rely on.</strong> Smart treadmills
            are heavy and most buyers pay for assembly. Read the warranty by component: a lifetime
            motor term next to one year of labour means the motor is covered but the visit to fit
            it may not be. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">warranty guide</a>
            explains how to read the terms.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What it is worth when you sell it.</strong> A smart treadmill's resale value
            depends heavily on whether its screen is still supported and whether the next owner
            will want the same platform. A machine with a standard Bluetooth connection and no
            platform dependency is easier to sell, which partly offsets its plainer console.
          </p>
          <p>
            Run that arithmetic and the ranking changes. A Bowflex T6 on sale plus a mid-priced app
            can cost less over five years than a T Series 10 with a full iFIT membership, even
            though the NordicTrack is the cheaper built-in screen. Neither answer is wrong — it
            depends on whether the platform is what keeps you training.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If you will not pay for a membership.</strong> Most of a built-in screen's
            value disappears without one, so you would be paying for a large display you rarely
            use. Buy a well-built treadmill with Bluetooth instead — the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">no-subscription roundup</a>
            has the picks.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you mainly run fast or far.</strong> Screens add cost without adding
            anything to the deck, motor or frame. A runner who trains by pace and heart rate is
            better served by spending the screen money on a sturdier machine; see our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-running/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">running treadmill guide</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you are committed to NordicTrack already.</strong> If the decision is which
            NordicTrack rather than which platform, our
            <a href="/best-nordictrack-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best NordicTrack treadmills</a>
            roundup compares the whole current range, including the incline trainers.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want Zwift above all.</strong> Look for a machine with a standard
            Bluetooth connection and a sturdy deck rather than a built-in platform; the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-zwift/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Zwift treadmill roundup</a>
            covers that route.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a smart treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A smart treadmill connects to software that does more than show speed and distance. Usually that means a built-in touchscreen running a content platform such as iFIT or Peloton, which can stream classes and change speed and incline for you. More loosely, it also covers treadmills that connect over Bluetooth to apps such as Zwift on your own tablet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do smart treadmills require a subscription?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The classes and automatic control on built-in platforms do, and that membership is an ongoing cost for as long as you use them. The treadmill itself usually still runs in a manual mode without it. Bowflex and Horizon machines need no subscription to work at all; you pay for an app only if you want one. Always check the current terms for the exact model.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a built-in screen better than using a tablet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is more integrated, not necessarily better. A built-in screen makes automatic speed and incline changes seamless and suits classes that move off the belt. A tablet is cheaper, gets replaced as technology moves on, and lets you change apps without changing treadmills. If automatic control matters most, choose a built-in platform; if flexibility matters most, choose a tablet setup.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use Zwift on a NordicTrack or Peloton?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Those machines are designed to run their own platforms, and connecting a third-party app ranges from awkward to unsupported depending on the model and software version. If Zwift matters to you, choose a treadmill with a standard Bluetooth fitness connection, such as the Bowflex T6 or Horizon 7.8 AT, or confirm compatibility for the exact model before buying.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens to a smart treadmill if the company stops supporting it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The motor, deck and frame keep working, and most machines retain a manual mode. What you lose is the screen-based content and, eventually, software updates. That is why it is worth weighting warranty terms and the underlying hardware heavily, and why tablet-based machines age more gracefully: you simply replace the tablet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best smart treadmill for a small apartment?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Of these six, the Echelon Stride-6s is the one built for it: it folds itself flat at the press of a button and stores in a small footprint, with a 10-inch touchscreen. The NordicTrack T Series 10 and Bowflex T6 also fold. Check the folded dimensions against your space and confirm the delivery route before ordering.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Smart Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these comes from treating the screen as the product instead of the treadmill underneath it and the bill that comes with it.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing sticker prices instead of five-year cost</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The membership on a built-in platform is an ongoing charge, and over five years it can exceed the hardware price. Add the annual membership for every year you expect to use classes before comparing a smart treadmill with a plainer machine and an app.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not asking what works without the membership</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Manual mode is not the same on every machine, and on some platforms a membership has been required at purchase. Ask the retailer or manufacturer to show you exactly what the console does with no active membership before you buy.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for a big screen on a weak drivetrain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The screen ages faster than the motor and frame beneath it. Check the deck size, the incline range, the maximum user weight and the warranty by component first, and treat the screen size as the last decision rather than the first.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming any app can control any treadmill</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Many app and treadmill pairings only read your speed; fewer let the app change speed and incline for you. If automatic control matters, confirm it for the exact pairing, or choose a built-in platform where it is part of the design.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Related Guides -->
        <div class="mt-16">
